 "pair": "ru-en",
 "title": "Храбрый круассан  (The Brave Croissant)",
 "level": "A1 — Duolingo Section 2",
 "labels": {
  "max": "Макс",
  "vika": "Вика"
 },
 "segments": [
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Max goes to the bar, where Vika has a drink waiting for him. In this kitchen, people are croissants or sticks of celery, and Max wants to know which he is.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "А я кто?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Круассан или сельдерей?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Ну, конечно, круассан.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Почему круассан?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я же не ем мучное.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Ты спас наш ресторан.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Ты молодец, Макс.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я просто сделал свою работу.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Нет, это было круто.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Ты очень помог мне.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Спасибо тебе.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Мой храбрый круассан.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Again, slowly.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "А я кто?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'And what am I?' Literally 'and I who?' Russian uses kto, who, for a person even here, where English says 'what'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Круассан или сельдерей?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'A croissant or celery?' 'Kruassan' is French and selderey is borrowed too, so both are easy to guess. 'Ili' means 'or'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Ну, конечно, круассан.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Well, of course a croissant.' Vika answers with the noun alone, no 'you are'. Nu, 'well', softens it.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Почему круассан?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Why a croissant?' 'Pochemu' is 'why'. Inside it is chemu, a form of chto, 'what'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я же не ем мучное.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I don't eat flour products.' 'Muchnoe', 'floury things', comes from muka, flour. Ne em is 'I don't eat', and zhe adds 'after all'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Ты спас наш ресторан.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'You saved our restaurant.' 'Spas', 'saved', is the same word Kostya used about Max. 'Nash restoran' doesn't change its ending here, even though it's the thing being saved.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Ты молодец, Макс.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'You did great, Max.' 'Molodets' again. Russians say ty molodets, 'you are a fine fellow', to anyone who did well.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Я просто сделал свою работу.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I just did my job.' 'Svoyu rabotu', 'my own job'. 'Svoy' means 'one's own' and replaces moy when it belongs to the subject.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Нет, это было круто.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'No, it was cool.' 'Kruto' is slang for 'cool', but its plain meaning is 'steeply', as in a steep hill.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Ты очень помог мне.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'You helped me a lot.' 'Pomog mne', 'helped to me', the same 'to' pattern as 'pomog Vike'. 'Ochen pomog', 'very helped', is how Russian says 'helped a lot'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Спасибо тебе.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Thank you.' 'Spasibo tebe', 'thanks to you', uses the 'to' form of ty. With someone you call vy, it's spasibo vam.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Мой храбрый круассан.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'My brave croissant.' 'Khrabryy', brave, has the masculine ending because kruassan is masculine. Now it's a pet name. Now the scene in one piece.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "А я кто?",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Круассан или сельдерей?",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Ну, конечно, круассан.",
   "gap": 0.5
